Navigate the OpenShift Web Console
Objective
Learn how to:
•	Access the OpenShift Web Console
•	Navigate its menus and perspectives
•	Identify applications and cluster services
________________________________________
1. What is the OpenShift Web Console
The OpenShift Web Console is a graphical user interface (GUI) used to manage an OpenShift cluster.
It allows administrators and developers to perform tasks such as:
•	deploying applications
•	monitoring pods
•	managing storage
•	configuring networking
•	viewing logs
•	installing operators
Instead of using command-line commands, users can perform tasks visually through a browser.
________________________________________
Important Idea
OpenShift Web Console uses:
•	Kubernetes APIs
•	OpenShift APIs
to perform operations.
So anything done in the web console can also be done using CLI.
Example CLI equivalents:
Check pods:
oc get pods
Check projects:
oc get projects
________________________________________
2. Difference Between Kubernetes Dashboard and OpenShift Console
Kubernetes has its own dashboard, but it is limited.
Kubernetes Dashboard
Problems:
•	Not installed by default
•	Uses token-based authentication
•	Requires proxy setup
•	Limited security features
________________________________________
OpenShift Web Console
Advantages:
•	installed by default
•	secure authentication
•	advanced UI
•	integrated cluster tools
•	extensible via operators
So the OpenShift console is much more powerful.
________________________________________
3. Accessing the OpenShift Web Console
To access the console you must:
1.	Login to cluster using CLI
2.	Get the console URL
3.	Open URL in browser
________________________________________
Step 1: Login to OpenShift
Command:
oc login -u <username> -p <password> <API_URL>
Example:
oc login -u developer -p developer https://api.ocp4.example.com:6443
Explanation:
Parameter	Meaning
-u	username
-p	password
API_URL	cluster API endpoint
If successful:
Login successful
________________________________________
Step 2: Get Console URL
Command:
oc whoami --show-console
Example output:
https://console-openshift-console.apps.ocp4.example.com
This URL is the OpenShift Web Console address.
________________________________________
Step 3: Open Browser
Open the URL in a web browser.
You will see the OpenShift login page.
After authentication you reach the OpenShift home page.
________________________________________
4. OpenShift Web Console Perspectives
The console has different perspectives for different users.
A perspective changes the menu layout.
Main perspectives:
1.	Administrator
2.	Developer
3.	Virtualization (optional)
________________________________________
Administrator Perspective
Used by cluster administrators.
Focus areas:
•	cluster configuration
•	infrastructure management
•	node management
•	networking
•	operators
•	security
Typical tasks:
•	install operators
•	manage nodes
•	monitor cluster
•	manage storage
________________________________________
Developer Perspective
Used by application developers.
Focus areas:
•	creating applications
•	deploying containers
•	viewing logs
•	monitoring application performance
Developers mainly interact with:
•	builds
•	deployments
•	routes
•	services
________________________________________
Virtualization Perspective
Appears only if OpenShift Virtualization Operator is installed.
Used for:
•	creating virtual machines
•	managing VM resources
________________________________________
5. Switching Perspectives
The perspective switcher is located at the top of the sidebar.
Example:
Administrator ▼
Developer
Virtualization
You can switch between them anytime.
________________________________________
6. Web Console Layout
The main navigation method is the sidebar menu.
Main sections include:
Home
Workloads
Networking
Storage
Builds
Operators
User Management
Administration
Each section contains different cluster features.
________________________________________
7. Home Section
The Home → Overview page provides a summary of the cluster.
Information shown:
•	cluster status
•	resource usage
•	health information
•	quick links
________________________________________
Home → Projects
Projects show all namespaces available to the user.
Remember:
OpenShift uses Projects instead of Namespaces.
Example CLI command:
oc get projects
________________________________________
8. OperatorHub
The OperatorHub is a catalog of operators.
Operators are applications that extend Kubernetes functionality.
Examples:
•	database operators
•	monitoring operators
•	service mesh operators
________________________________________
Accessing OperatorHub
Menu:
Operators → OperatorHub
Here you can:
•	search operators
•	install operators
•	extend cluster capabilities
________________________________________
9. Developer Catalog
The Developer Catalog allows developers to deploy applications easily.
It contains:
•	templates
•	application services
•	event sources
•	Source-to-Image builders
Example items:
•	Node.js applications
•	databases
•	messaging systems
________________________________________
Workflow Example
Developer selects:
Node.js template
OpenShift automatically:
Create build
Create container image
Deploy application
Create route
________________________________________
10. Key OpenShift Concepts in Web Console
When using the web console, you will frequently see these terms.
________________________________________
Pods
Definition:
A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes.
A pod contains:
•	one or more containers
Example CLI command:
oc get pods
________________________________________
Deployments
A Deployment manages application pods.
Responsibilities:
•	create pods
•	update pods
•	scale pods
Example:
oc get deployments
Scaling example:
oc scale deployment nginx --replicas=3
________________________________________
Projects
Projects are OpenShift namespaces with additional features.
Used for:
•	multitenancy
•	resource isolation
•	access control
Command:
oc new-project myproject
List projects:
oc get projects
________________________________________
Routes
Routes expose applications outside the cluster.
They provide an external URL.
Example:
https://myapp.apps.cluster.com
Command:
oc get routes
Create route:
oc expose service myapp
________________________________________
Operators
Operators automate application management.
Example:
Database Operator can automatically:
•	install database
•	configure replication
•	manage backups
Check operators:
oc get operators
